ks) a)  Negative feedback. (1)  b)  Any four  Maintains constant blood glucose. Prevents excessively high blood sugar. Prevents dangerously low blood sugar. Ensures normal cell respiration. Maintains homeostasis.  (4 ×1)  9. Distinguish between Insulin and Glucagon (8 Marks) Feature   Insulin Glucagon Produced by    Beta cells (1)  Alpha cells (1) Released when   Blood sugar rises (1)   Blood sugar falls (1) Effect    Lowers blood sugar (1)  Raises blood sugar (1) Liver action     Converts glucose to glycogen (1)        Breaks glycogen into glucose (1)  Total: 8 Marks  10. Student Who Skipped Breakfast (6 Marks)  Award 1 mark for each point.  Blood glucose falls. Alpha cells detect decrease. Glucagon is released. Liver converts glycogen to glucose. Glucose released into blood. Blood glucose returns to normal. 11. Diabetes Mellitus (8 Marks) a) Definition (2 marks) Disease in which blood glucose remains abnormally high (1) Due to insufficient insulin production or ineffective insulin action (1) b) Symptoms (4 marks)  Any four  Frequent urination Excessive thirst Weight loss Constant hunger Fatigue Blurred vision Slow wound healing  (4 ×1)  c) Why insulin injections? (2 marks) Replace missing insulin (1) Help control blood glucose concentration (1) 12. Removal of Excess Glucose (4 Marks)  Any four  Converted into glycogen in liver. Converted into glycogen in muscles. Used during respiration. Converted into fats. Used in synthesis of other compounds.  (4 ×1)  13. Importance of Blood Sugar Regulation (8 Marks)  Award 2 marks each  Any four  Maintains constant internal environment. Ensures continuous supply of glucose for respiration. Prevents hyperglycaemia. Prevents hypoglycaemia. Protects the brain from glucose shortage. Maintains efficient enzyme activity.  (4 ×2)  14. Multiple Choice (5 Marks) Question     Answer 1        C. Insulin 2    C. Liver 3      C. Falls below normal 4 B. Beta cells 5 B. Glycogenesis  (5 Marks)  15. Essay Question (16 Marks) a) After eating 